A clip that does not play moves the chain on.

dsh-pet reads its clips from disk; A-NAS fetches them from the NAS, so a
fetch can fail, time out or get an HTTP error, and a clip can fail to decode
or to play. The chain only goes on at a clip's end, and a switch whose clip
did not come was only released (patch 0029 handed on an end held back while
it loaded, which covers neither case below). The pet then stayed as it was:
asleep for good when the clip it wakes with failed, as sleep's loop never
ends while VNAS had been told it was awake, and still on the last frame when
the clip after one that had ended failed, until something else moved it.

After a clip that does not play, the chain waits a second, fetches it once
more (not a command's, which is answered already), and if that fails too
goes on as if the clip had ended: the sleep machine takes its next step, the
random chain picks the next clip. A clip in front that still plays goes on
at its own end. The wait doubles for every clip in a row that does not play,
up to 30 s, so a NAS out of reach is not asked again and again; a clip that
plays starts it over.

